WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.814
Welcome to Module 6 of the
Ticket-to-Machine learning path:

2
00:00:03.814 --> 00:00:05.775
actuation and the re-read rule.

3
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:08.825
This module covers the
guide's Gates 5 and 6,

4
00:00:08.825 --> 00:00:11.700
and the developer workbook's Chapter 05.

5
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.062
In Module 5, an event became a
finding with an intended state.

6
00:00:16.062 --> 00:00:19.274
Now the fix has to happen,
and the finding has to close.

7
00:00:19.274 --> 00:00:22.975
You'll see where the fix runs on
these products, lane by lane.

8
00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:27.868
Then you'll see how a ServiceNow business
rule and a re-read client enforce one

9
00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:32.838
rule: a finding closes only when a re-read
of the target shows the intended state.

10
00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:37.350
In the lab, you'll watch the client
decide six waiting findings on two days.

11
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.720
The code is illustrative, tested
against fictional fixtures.

12
00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:46.620
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.375
SailPoint's Service Desk
integration treats a provisioning

14
00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:53.982
task as complete when the mapped
ServiceNow status says so.

15
00:00:53.982 --> 00:00:57.645
So a technician sets a ticket to
Closed, and nothing was checked.

16
00:00:57.645 --> 00:00:59.670
The account might still be enabled.

17
00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.208
The group might still be there.

18
00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:05.565
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 13's rule is different:

19
00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:07.744
a success response isn't closure.

20
00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:12.570
A finding closes only when a re-read of
the target shows the intended state.

21
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.799
Gate 6 enforces that in ServiceNow,

22
00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:19.795
for identity findings and for the tickets
the Service Desk integration opens.

23
00:01:19.795 --> 00:01:22.300
In this module you see where the fix runs,

24
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.530
and then you enforce the rule and watch
the re-read client decide six findings.

25
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.120
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

26
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.767
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

27
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:41.829
You'll explain how Gate 5 actuates by
lane, and why the kit has no test for it.

28
00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:44.747
You'll describe what the
business rule does with a close,

29
00:01:44.747 --> 00:01:46.142
and who can close a finding.

30
00:01:46.142 --> 00:01:50.797
You'll run the re-read client offline
and interpret its four outcomes:

31
00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:53.492
verified, mismatch, pending and skipped.

32
00:01:53.492 --> 00:01:56.184
And you'll explain why a stale read waits,

33
00:01:56.184 --> 00:01:59.529
and why an empty intended
state is never verified.

34
00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:04.307
Before you start, finish Module 5, and
review Module 2's ServiceNow roles,

35
00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:06.655
because the verifier is one of them.

36
00:02:06.655 --> 00:02:08.355
Lab 1 needs no tenant.

37
00:02:08.355 --> 00:02:12.083
Lab 2 needs your developer
instance, the verifier user,

38
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:14.920
and a test user in a test Entra tenant.

39
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.770
Gate 5 is where the ticket
authorizes a machine.

40
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.216
The fix runs through a connector
under a service identity,

41
00:02:25.216 --> 00:02:27.370
and a person's role stops at approval.

42
00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.658
There are four lanes.

43
00:02:28.658 --> 00:02:33.920
SailPoint's connectors provision
owner-governed access: creating, updating,

44
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.880
enabling, disabling and deleting accounts,

45
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.745
and changing groups, roles,
licenses and access packages.

46
00:02:40.745 --> 00:02:43.595
When the Entra connector
disables an account,

47
00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:46.520
it also signs the user
out of existing sessions.

48
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.201
ServiceNow's Entra spoke
handles low-authority tasks

49
00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:53.882
where its actions cover them;
it has no MFA-reset action.

50
00:02:53.882 --> 00:02:57.516
Disconnected applications
run as declared exceptions,

51
00:02:57.516 --> 00:03:01.071
with a named approver, a recorded
session and an end date.

52
00:03:01.071 --> 00:03:04.895
And people act only through a
just-in-time PIM activation.

53
00:03:04.895 --> 00:03:08.780
The kit's Pass 1 doesn't actuate,
so Gate 5 is configuration

54
00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.940
of the products themselves,
with no automated check yet.

55
00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.802
Gate 6 starts with a business rule.

56
00:03:18.802 --> 00:03:21.354
It runs before update, on the state field,

57
00:03:21.354 --> 00:03:24.600
and it turns any close into
Awaiting verification,

58
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.778
unless the same update carries new evidence
and comes from the verifier account.

59
00:03:29.778 --> 00:03:31.140
Walk through the cases.

60
00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.365
A person sets the state
to Closed: redirected.

61
00:03:34.365 --> 00:03:38.554
An update that carries new evidence
but comes from someone else:

62
00:03:38.554 --> 00:03:41.715
redirected, because evidence
alone isn't enough.

63
00:03:41.715 --> 00:03:44.666
The verifier account without new evidence:

64
00:03:44.666 --> 00:03:48.190
redirected, because the
verifier alone isn't enough.

65
00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:51.940
The verifier with a new evidence
hash: the close stands.

66
00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.672
And a named exception approver can
still cancel with a close note,

67
00:03:55.672 --> 00:03:57.815
which is the declared-exception lane.

68
00:03:57.815 --> 00:04:00.677
The rule covers the
identity finding table and,

69
00:04:00.677 --> 00:04:05.081
on the incident and request item
tables, the tickets SailPoint's Service

70
00:04:05.081 --> 00:04:10.150
Desk integration opened, so its status
mapping no longer decides completion.

71
00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.987
The re-read client does the checking.

72
00:04:15.987 --> 00:04:20.208
It lists the waiting findings
through ServiceNow's Table API,

73
00:04:20.208 --> 00:04:22.938
then re-reads each subject by its system.

74
00:04:22.938 --> 00:04:25.750
An ISC account is read by its ID.

75
00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.663
An ISC identity is found with a Search.

76
00:04:28.663 --> 00:04:32.431
And an Entra user is read
through Microsoft Graph,

77
00:04:32.431 --> 00:04:35.925
which returns a 404 when
the user doesn't exist.

78
00:04:35.925 --> 00:04:39.625
Each read has its own least
permission, shown on the slide.

79
00:04:39.625 --> 00:04:43.475
Then the client compares what it read
with the finding's intended state.

80
00:04:43.475 --> 00:04:48.551
On a match, it closes the record with
the evidence: the query, the time,

81
00:04:48.551 --> 00:04:53.442
a SHA-256 hash of the response,
the intended state and the checker,

82
00:04:53.442 --> 00:04:56.487
and it attaches that evidence as JSON.

83
00:04:56.487 --> 00:05:00.200
On a mismatch, it reopens the
record with the differences.

84
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.420
And it writes nothing unless
you pass the apply option.

85
00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.895
Two kinds of read need care.

86
00:05:08.895 --> 00:05:10.470
First, a stale read.

87
00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:15.032
An ISC read reflects the last
aggregation, not the source right now.

88
00:05:15.032 --> 00:05:18.685
So if the read is older than the
time the finding started waiting,

89
00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:19.995
it can't show the fix yet.

90
00:05:19.995 --> 00:05:23.344
The client calls it pending,
and waits for a grace period,

91
00:05:23.344 --> 00:05:26.620
twenty-four hours by default,
which is design guidance.

92
00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:31.133
After the grace period, a read that
still disagrees is a mismatch.

93
00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:35.843
Tune the grace period to the longest
aggregation interval on your sources,

94
00:05:35.843 --> 00:05:39.870
and record the setting; how long
the lag really is wasn't verified.

95
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.620
Second, a missing read.

96
00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:45.045
A subject nobody read must
never count as a match.

97
00:05:45.045 --> 00:05:49.107
In offline mode, a subject with
no saved read is an error.

98
00:05:49.107 --> 00:05:52.770
The live reader raises
on anything but a 404.

99
00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:56.480
And a saved read of null
means the read found nothing.

100
00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.743
Chapter 05 adds two things.

101
00:06:02.743 --> 00:06:03.980
The first is a guard.

102
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:08.368
The client already skipped a record whose
intended state was completely empty.

103
00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:12.219
But it didn't skip one that was a
non-empty object naming nothing,

104
00:06:12.219 --> 00:06:16.293
with empty attributes and an empty
list of entitlements to remove.

105
00:06:16.293 --> 00:06:19.209
That's exactly what the
script stores for a native

106
00:06:19.209 --> 00:06:22.268
change the workflow didn't
describe, the Module 5 gap.

107
00:06:22.268 --> 00:06:24.916
Comparing any read with it always matches,

108
00:06:24.916 --> 00:06:29.230
so the client would have closed that
record, with evidence, on nothing.

109
00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.620
Now, before reading anything, the
client checks the intended state, and a

110
00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:38.530
record that names nothing checkable is
skipped with the reason, never verified.

111
00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.605
The second is an offline mode.

112
00:06:40.605 --> 00:06:44.304
It takes saved waiting records,
saved reads and a fixed clock,

113
00:06:44.304 --> 00:06:47.855
and makes the same decisions
with only the reader swapped.

114
00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:51.860
It's a dry run only; the
apply option is refused.

115
00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.742
The kit comes with six
fictional waiting records,

116
00:06:58.742 --> 00:07:01.722
one per outcome, and the
lab runs them on two days.

117
00:07:01.722 --> 00:07:05.434
The first is a reverted native
change, and it verifies.

118
00:07:05.434 --> 00:07:10.597
The second is a terminated user still
enabled in Entra ID: a mismatch.

119
00:07:10.597 --> 00:07:14.497
The third is an ISC account read
before it was re-aggregated.

120
00:07:14.497 --> 00:07:19.198
At noon on November first it's pending;
by noon the next day it's a mismatch.

121
00:07:19.198 --> 00:07:22.272
The fourth, an attribute change, verifies.

122
00:07:22.272 --> 00:07:25.704
The fifth is a native change
with an empty intended state,

123
00:07:25.704 --> 00:07:27.384
and it's skipped by the guard.

124
00:07:27.384 --> 00:07:31.097
The sixth, an orphan account
that was disabled, verifies.

125
00:07:31.097 --> 00:07:32.973
Why does the third one change?

126
00:07:32.973 --> 00:07:36.427
It started waiting at six in
the morning on November first,

127
00:07:36.427 --> 00:07:40.817
so by noon on November second its
twenty-four-hour grace period has passed,

128
00:07:40.817 --> 00:07:43.690
and the stale read now
counts as a mismatch.

129
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.165
Try it.

130
00:07:48.165 --> 00:07:52.815
Lab 1 runs in the kit's servicenow-sailpoint
folder, with no tenant.

131
00:07:52.815 --> 00:07:56.515
Step one runs the re-read
tests, and fifteen pass.

132
00:07:56.515 --> 00:08:00.541
Step two runs the client offline
over the six waiting records,

133
00:08:00.541 --> 00:08:03.540
with the clock fixed at
noon on November first.

134
00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:04.690
Read the output.

135
00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:09.102
Three records verify, and the client
would patch each one to closed.

136
00:08:09.102 --> 00:08:14.127
The terminated user is a mismatch: the
intended state says account enabled false,

137
00:08:14.127 --> 00:08:18.627
and the read says true, so the client
would reopen it with that difference.

138
00:08:18.627 --> 00:08:23.394
The third record is pending, because
the ISC copy predates the change,

139
00:08:23.394 --> 00:08:25.903
so re-run after the next aggregation.

140
00:08:25.903 --> 00:08:31.265
The fifth is skipped, because its intended
state names nothing a re-read can check.

141
00:08:31.265 --> 00:08:34.127
Every line says what the
client would do and why.

142
00:08:34.127 --> 00:08:36.262
It's a dry run, so nothing is written,

143
00:08:36.262 --> 00:08:39.820
and pending and skipped
records are never patched.

144
00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:48.138
Step three runs the same records a
day later, at noon on November second,

145
00:08:48.138 --> 00:08:53.258
and keeps the results in a JSON file in
an out folder, which the client creates.

146
00:08:53.258 --> 00:08:56.771
On the slide, the five
unchanged lines are trimmed.

147
00:08:56.771 --> 00:08:59.058
The one that changed is the third record.

148
00:08:59.058 --> 00:09:03.313
Its grace period has passed, so
the stale read is now a mismatch:

149
00:09:03.313 --> 00:09:07.569
the intended state says disabled
true, and the read says false.

150
00:09:07.569 --> 00:09:12.145
The summary is now two mismatches,
one skipped and three verified.

151
00:09:12.145 --> 00:09:15.957
The JSON file holds each
result with its evidence hash.

152
00:09:15.957 --> 00:09:20.952
Step four runs the business rule's six
tests, and they read like the rule itself:

153
00:09:20.952 --> 00:09:25.711
a person's close is redirected, the
verifier with fresh evidence may close,

154
00:09:25.711 --> 00:09:30.392
evidence alone or the verifier alone
isn't enough, an exception approver

155
00:09:30.392 --> 00:09:35.308
may cancel with a note, and only tickets
the integration opened are governed.

156
00:09:35.308 --> 00:09:37.870
Step five deletes the out folder.

157
00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:45.845
Lab 2 enforces the rule in your
own ServiceNow developer instance.

158
00:09:45.845 --> 00:09:48.775
You need the instance
from Modules 1 and 2,

159
00:09:48.775 --> 00:09:54.033
the verifier user from Module 2's Lab 2,
and a test user in a test Entra tenant.

160
00:09:54.033 --> 00:09:57.308
Configure the business rule
and its system properties.

161
00:09:57.308 --> 00:10:01.845
Close a finding as a person, and watch
it land in Awaiting verification.

162
00:10:01.845 --> 00:10:06.770
Then confirm which user ServiceNow
records for the verifier's OAuth client.

163
00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:10.837
That matters, because the rule compares
it with the verifier property,

164
00:10:10.837 --> 00:10:12.870
and the workbook couldn't verify it.

165
00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:17.770
If it's wrong, every close is refused,
and people look for a way around the rule.

166
00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:22.845
Finally, close a finding with evidence
from a Graph read of a disabled test user.

167
00:10:22.845 --> 00:10:26.562
Then back it out: delete the test
findings and their attachments,

168
00:10:26.562 --> 00:10:30.770
deactivate the rule if you built it
for the lab, and delete the properties.

169
00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.280
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

170
00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.092
What differs in the government clouds?

171
00:10:39.092 --> 00:10:42.980
The guide's availability notes
for Gates 5 and 6 answer it.

172
00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:46.192
PIM is available in
Azure for U S Government.

173
00:10:46.192 --> 00:10:51.092
ServiceNow's Entra spoke points at
the worldwide Graph host by default,

174
00:10:51.092 --> 00:10:54.746
and its page doesn't mention
the U S Government clouds,

175
00:10:54.746 --> 00:10:59.230
so it fits GCC, but for GCC High,
confirm with ServiceNow before use.

176
00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:03.455
SailPoint's connector endpoints
for GCC High are documented.

177
00:11:03.455 --> 00:11:08.608
For the re-read itself, GCC uses
the worldwide Graph endpoint,

178
00:11:08.608 --> 00:11:12.318
and GCC High uses graph
dot microsoft dot u s.

179
00:11:12.318 --> 00:11:16.905
And the ISC API domain for a
FedRAMP tenant wasn't confirmed.

180
00:11:16.905 --> 00:11:18.417
There are interim paths.

181
00:11:18.417 --> 00:11:23.405
Until the change-record flow exists, the
approver raises the change record by hand.

182
00:11:23.405 --> 00:11:28.313
And until the re-read client runs on a
schedule, the verifier runs it by hand

183
00:11:28.313 --> 00:11:33.340
once a day, dry run first; the business
rule still blocks a person's close.

184
00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:38.477
The federal requirement.

185
00:11:38.477 --> 00:11:43.757
The guide's Gate 5 table maps actuation
to NIST special publication eight hundred

186
00:11:43.757 --> 00:11:49.037
fifty-three: access restrictions for
change, access enforcement, least privilege,

187
00:11:49.037 --> 00:11:54.316
change control, session audit and the plan
of action and milestones, and to special

188
00:11:54.316 --> 00:11:59.440
publication eight hundred sixty-three B,
revision four, for resets and recovery.

189
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.635
The Gate 6 table maps the
re-read to change control,

190
00:12:02.635 --> 00:12:07.778
verification of controls, continuous
monitoring and audit record generation.

191
00:12:07.778 --> 00:12:10.490
Chapter 05 adds separation of duties:

192
00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:14.078
no person can close a
finding by setting its state.

193
00:12:14.078 --> 00:12:19.791
The evidence an ISSO keeps: connector and
spoke identities with their permissions,

194
00:12:19.791 --> 00:12:24.676
provisioning events, activation
records and change records for Gate 5;

195
00:12:24.676 --> 00:12:29.395
and for Gate 6, the evidence attachment
and hash on each closed record,

196
00:12:29.395 --> 00:12:33.866
the re-read result and time, and
the verifier account's audit trail.

197
00:12:33.866 --> 00:12:37.920
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 05 checklist.

198
00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:43.645
Check yourself with two questions.

199
00:12:43.645 --> 00:12:48.483
First: at noon on November first, record
one thousand and three is pending.

200
00:12:48.483 --> 00:12:49.170
Why?

201
00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.307
The answer is a.

202
00:12:50.307 --> 00:12:53.336
The ISC copy of the account
predates the change,

203
00:12:53.336 --> 00:12:56.208
so the client waits for
the next aggregation.

204
00:12:56.208 --> 00:12:59.195
After the grace period,
it becomes a mismatch.

205
00:12:59.195 --> 00:13:03.108
Second: under the business rule,
which update can close a finding?

206
00:13:03.108 --> 00:13:08.071
The answer is c: an update from the
verifier account that carries new evidence.

207
00:13:08.071 --> 00:13:11.907
Evidence alone, or the
verifier alone, isn't enough.

208
00:13:11.907 --> 00:13:14.566
The module page has two more questions,

209
00:13:14.566 --> 00:13:19.070
on a missing saved read and on why
the kit has no test for Gate 5.

210
00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:23.645
To sum up.

211
00:13:23.645 --> 00:13:28.349
You learned where actuation runs on
these products: SailPoint's connectors,

212
00:13:28.349 --> 00:13:31.709
ServiceNow's Entra spoke,
the declared-exception lane,

213
00:13:31.709 --> 00:13:34.695
and people only through
just-in-time activation.

214
00:13:34.695 --> 00:13:37.118
You saw how the business
rule and the re-read

215
00:13:37.118 --> 00:13:39.945
client keep a ticket status
from closing a finding.

216
00:13:39.945 --> 00:13:45.383
You saw how the client treats stale reads,
missing reads and empty intended states.

217
00:13:45.383 --> 00:13:48.595
And you ran it offline over
six findings on two days.

218
00:13:48.595 --> 00:13:52.295
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 05 checklist.

219
00:13:52.295 --> 00:13:57.758
Next is Module 7, the last in
the path: evidence for the ISSO.

220
00:13:57.758 --> 00:14:00.720
You'll learn what Gate 7 runs and reports,

221
00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:04.672
export closed findings as
Book 13's evidence records,

222
00:14:04.672 --> 00:14:10.030
chain one period to the next, and verify
the package the way an ISSO would.
